WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.030
Analytic geometry is one of the main branches
of

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.850
higher mathematics, in which geometric
problems

3
00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.810
are solved by algebraic methods.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.539
The basic notions of analytic geometry are
points,

5
00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:17.380
lines, planes, and curves and surfaces

6
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.480
of the second order.

7
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:28.720
The main method of analytic geometry is the
coordinate method: here

8
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.460
the properties of geometric objects are
studied by algebraic methods

9
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:38.120
using their equations. In this lesson

10
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.100
we will look at the kinds of lines in the
plane.

11
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.717
The equation F(x, y) = 0 is called the
equation

12
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:49.800
of a line, or curve, in the Oxy plane

13
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.120
if the coordinates x and y of every point M on
this line

14
00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:02.720
satisfy the equation, and the coordinates of
any point not on the line

15
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.910
do not satisfy it.

16
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:12.430
A point M(x, y) moving along the line is
called the current point, and

17
00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:17.930
its coordinates x and y are called the current
coordinates.

18
00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.213
A straight line is a geometric figure made of

19
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:27.390
points. Each point has its own coordinates

20
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:34.360
along the x- and y-axes. The equation
describing how the coordinates of each

21
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.249
point of a line in a Cartesian system are
related

22
00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:40.080
is called the equation of the line in the
plane.

23
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:49.340
The equation Ax + By + C = 0 is the general
equation of a line.

24
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:56.150
Here A and B do not vanish at the same time,
that is,

25
00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:02.860
A² + B² ≠ 0. The coefficients A and B

26
00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:09.940
give the coordinates of the normal vector, a
vector perpendicular to the line.

27
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:17.640
Look at the picture: the line in the picture
is given by the equation

28
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:23.290
x + 2y − 2 = 0. The coordinates of every point
that makes up the line

29
00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.910
satisfy this equation. Here

30
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:36.090
the set of points given by the equation forms
a straight line, as the picture shows.

31
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.062
The general equation of a line can be complete

32
00:02:41.062 --> 00:02:44.210
or incomplete. In a complete equation all

33
00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:51.130
the numbers A, B and C are nonzero; in all
other cases the equation

34
00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:58.080
is considered incomplete. An equation of the
form Ax + By = 0 defines a line

35
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.580
passing through the origin.

36
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.980
If only A = 0, the line given by By + C = 0

37
00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:18.660
is parallel to the Ox axis, or y = b, where b
= −C/B.

38
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:27.250
If only B = 0, the line given by Ax + C = 0

39
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:36.090
is parallel to the Oy axis, or x = a, where a
= −C/A.

40
00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:44.930
If a line is given by Ax = 0, or x = 0, then

41
00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:51.890
it coincides with the Oy axis. If a line is
given by By = 0, or

42
00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:57.690
y = 0, it coincides with the Ox axis.

43
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.786
All the equations of a line considered below

44
00:04:02.786 --> 00:04:05.980
can be obtained from the general equation,

45
00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:12.420
and the reverse is also possible: any of these
equations of a line

46
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.860
can be brought to the general equation.

47
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:25.060
In a rectangular coordinate system, let a
point M₀ with coordinates x₀, y₀ and

48
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.760
a vector n with coordinates A, B be given.

49
00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:35.040
Through this point we draw a line
perpendicular to the normal vector n.

50
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.720
We mark any point M on the line. This gives
another equation of the line:

51
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:48.060
from the condition that vectors n and M₀M are
perpendicular,

52
00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:54.750
we get the equation of a line given by a
normal vector. Expanding the brackets,

53
00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.910
we obtain the general equation of the line.

54
00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.070
Let us write one more equation of a line.

55
00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:07.740
To do this, we again draw a line in the
coordinate plane. It cuts off on the Oy axis

56
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.500
a segment equal to b and makes with the Ox
axis

57
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:14.620
an angle φ not equal to ninety degrees.

58
00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:21.220
This angle is called the angle of inclination,
and the tangent of this angle

59
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.830
is called the slope of the line and is denoted

60
00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.970
by the letter k. Using these quantities,

61
00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:34.670
we can write the equation of a line with
slope: y = kx + b.

62
00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:41.270
If we express y in terms of x in the general
equation of a line,

63
00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:48.250
it can be written as an equation with slope.
Hence k equals

64
00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.230
−A/B.

65
00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.080
Let us work through an example.

66
00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:06.863
We need to write the equation of a line that
cuts off a segment of five on the Oy

67
00:06:06.863 --> 00:06:16.980
axis and makes an angle of one hundred
thirty-five degrees with the Ox axis.

68
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:28.620
We have the formula y = kx + b; by the
conditions b = 5, and the angle φ equals

69
00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.762
one hundred thirty-five degrees.

70
00:06:30.762 --> 00:06:35.580
By definition k = tan φ, that is, the tangent
of one hundred thirty-five

71
00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:38.980
degrees, which equals minus one.

72
00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.112
Let us look at one more equation of a line.

73
00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:47.640
To do this, we again draw a line in the
coordinate plane,

74
00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:54.510
inclined to the Ox axis at an angle φ, and
mark on it a point M₀ with coordinates x₀

75
00:06:54.510 --> 00:07:01.350
and y₀. Using these quantities,

76
00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.861
we can write the equation of a line through

77
00:07:04.861 --> 00:07:08.290
a given point in a given direction: y − y₀

78
00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:13.570
equals k times the difference of x and x₀.

79
00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.220
Let us write the equation of the line through
the point M₀

80
00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.520
with coordinates two, three that makes with
the Ox axis

81
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:29.940
an angle of inclination of sixty degrees. We
find the

82
00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:35.500
slope: the tangent of sixty degrees equals the
square root of three.

83
00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.068
Now we substitute into the formula,

84
00:07:39.068 --> 00:07:42.900
expand the brackets and finally get the
following

85
00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.500
equation of the line.

86
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:54.250
Now let us write the equation of a line
through two given points. Let the line pass

87
00:07:54.250 --> 00:08:00.930
through two given points M₁ and M₂, where x₁ ≠
x₂

88
00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.730
and y₁ ≠ y₂.

89
00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:13.020
M is any point of this line. Then the equation
of the line through two points

90
00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.380
has the following form.

91
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.546
Let us write the equation of the line through
the points M₁ with

92
00:08:21.546 --> 00:08:24.860
coordinates two, three and M₂ with coordinates
six, minus four.

93
00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:31.380
We substitute the given numbers into the
formula

94
00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.560
and get the following relation.

95
00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:39.150
The problem does not say which equation to
write,

96
00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:45.150
so we find the general equation. To do this,
we set up a proportion.

97
00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:52.280
Now we expand the brackets and move all the
terms to the left-hand side.

98
00:08:52.280 --> 00:09:02.100
Finally we get the general equation of the
line: 7x + 4y − 26 = 0.

99
00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:09.950
Let a line cut off segments equal to a and b
on the coordinate axes,

100
00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:17.490
that is, pass through the points with
coordinates (a; 0) and (0; b).

101
00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:22.008
Then we can write the intercept form of the
line's

102
00:09:22.008 --> 00:09:25.000
equation, but in this equation a and b

103
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.388
are nonzero. From this equation we can derive
the

104
00:09:28.388 --> 00:09:31.500
general equation of the line and the equation

105
00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.100
with slope.

106
00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:41.961
Look at the picture: the line cuts off a
segment

107
00:09:41.961 --> 00:09:45.140
of four on the Ox axis and on the Oy axis

108
00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:50.640
a segment of three.

109
00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.880
We get x/4 + y/3 = 1.

110
00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.114
From this equation we derive the general
equation

111
00:10:01.114 --> 00:10:03.960
of the line. To do this, we multiply

112
00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:06.620
both sides of the equation by twelve and,

113
00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:11.420
after the necessary transformations, get the
general equation of the line.

114
00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:21.620
As you can see, there are different ways to
write the equation of a line. This

115
00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.682
is explained by the nature of the problems
being solved

116
00:10:25.682 --> 00:10:29.080
and the ways a line can be given in the plane.
